Run one capability now. Saved goal:<slug> capabilities re-plan against the current environment, heal API changes and repeat their stored result checks and independent witnesses. Returns output, timing, receipt, verification and witness evidence. Optional predicates add checks; they never remove stored checks. A failed call to a learned API re-reads its contract once and retries the same operation if the API moved; an id that re-learning pointed at a different operation is refused until called with acknowledgeChange: true. Large outputs: pass view to get a count, field projection or slice instead of the whole payload.

exec tool declaration:
```ts
declare const tools: { mcp__rightclick_x__context_run(args: {
  // Run an id whose operation changed when its API was re-learned, after reading the change.
  acknowledgeChange?: boolean;
  // Input arguments by port name.
  args?: { [key: string]: unknown; };
  // Required for capabilities labelled destructive.
  confirm?: boolean;
  // Capability id.
  id: string;
  // Outcome checks on the result, e.g. [{"op":"contains","value":"Lisbon"}, {"op":"gt","path":"temperature","value":-50}]. ops: exists, equals, contains, matches, type, gt, lt, length_gt. Passing them makes a result "checked"; only an independent witness makes it "verified".
  verify?: Array<{ [key: string]: unknown; }>;
  // What to return of the output: a count, a projection or a slice. Without a view, an output over 32000 characters of JSON is withheld whole (never cut) and summarised by size, count and shape. The run record and receipt digest always cover the full output.
  view?: {
  // Return only the number of elements, keys or characters of the selected value.
  count?: boolean;
  // Project each element onto these paths, e.g. ["accession","sequence.length"].
  fields?: Array<string>;
  // Return the selected value whole, even when it is over the response budget.
  full?: boolean;
  // Length of a slice of the selected array.
  limit?: number;
  // Start of a slice of the selected array (default 0).
  offset?: number;
  // Path into the output, e.g. "items", "0.sequence.length", or "isoforms.accession" to map over an array.
  select?: string;
};
}): Promise<CallToolResult>; };
```"},{"name":"mcp__rightclick_x__context_run_status","description":"RightClick X is a live, self-composing capability graph. Start with context_research (optionally with a topic) to see what this environment can do, context_learn to add any URL, context_compose to turn what you have into what you want across providers, and context_run to execute. The tool list never changes; the graph behind it does.

